  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "4QJaDjlIEZlUr4+xqB4oU0Uw5Jw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:58.853Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The former everis, now NTT Data Spain, is credited with the strongest private-sector bench in Spain and a real OutSystems practice active across Spain, the UK, Portugal, Belgium and Italy, alongside Microsoft and SAP. The screen still rules it out, on ownership: it is foreign-owned.\n\nTwo criteria stack here, and it is worth separating them. The OutSystems practice is slot occupancy \u2014 the same disqualifier that demoted IG&H and ITSector, since a proprietary app-layer platform leaves no gap to fill. Foreign ownership is the one the note actually leads with, and it recurs across the corpus: the Japanese ownership of KMD and the absorption of Truewind into a French infrastructure group are recorded the same way.\n\nThe reason ownership is load-bearing rather than cosmetic is that the thesis being sold is sovereignty \u2014 client-owned infrastructure, local-first deployment, European procurement. A partner whose own decisions route through a parent outside the region is a weaker carrier of that argument, whatever the quality of its bench. Delivery strength does not compensate, which is why the strongest private-sector bench in the country sits at priority 9.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- NTT Data Spain (ex-everis) (ES)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Strongest private-sector bench in Spain and a real OutSystems practice, but foreign-owned.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:27:58.853685Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:58.853689Z"
    }
  },
